     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-11-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Russia Reports Drone Hit on Industrial Site Near Moscow, Ukraine Claims Refinery Strike</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#World News</span> <span>#Russia</span> <span>#Ukraine</span> <span>#Drone Attack</span></div>
       <p>Russia announced on Sunday, November 1, 2026, that a drone had struck an &quot;industrial enterprise&quot; located east of Moscow overnight, as Ukraine claimed responsibility for hitting a major oil refinery. Gleb Nikitin, the governor of the Nizhny Novgorod region, confirmed a &quot;massive drone attack&quot; during the night, indicating that an &quot;industrial enterprise in the Kstovo district sustained significant damage,&quot; according to The Hindu World.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-01/russia-reports-drone-hit-on-industrial-site-near-moscow-ukra.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Governor Nikitin further reported that &quot;More than 100 drones&quot; were destroyed in the attack, and &quot;according to preliminary information, there are no casualties.&quot; He did not, however, specify the exact activities of the plant that was hit. In contrast, Ukrainian media outlets identified the target as the Kstovo oil refinery, describing it as &quot;one of Russia's largest oil refineries, producing fuel and lubricants,&quot; thereby suggesting a strategic target for Kyiv.</p><p>The incident follows a statement from Moscow on Friday, October 30, 2026, indicating that Ukraine had &quot;resumed strikes on Russian oil refineries&quot; after a &quot;pause&quot; of several days. Senior Russian official Alexander Novak corroborated this, informing state TV that Ukraine had recommenced attacks on Russian plants, successfully hitting three oil refineries and one gas-chemical complex in recent operations.</p><p>These actions are part of Kyiv's broader strategy of retaliating against Russian oil and gas sites. Ukraine has consistently targeted such infrastructure in response to Russia's attacks on Ukrainian energy infrastructure, which have been ongoing since Moscow initiated its offensive in February 2022. Notably, former U.S. President Donald Trump had reportedly been pressuring Kyiv for weeks to halt these attacks, attributing them to the rising cost of diesel fuel.</p><p>What to watch: The impact of continued refinery strikes on global energy markets.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a thorough account of the incident and surrounding context, accurately reflecting the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/russia-says-drone-hit-industrial-enterprise-near-moscow-kyiv-claims-hit-major-refinery/article71653925.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
