     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · ScienceDaily · 2026-11-01 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Scientists Shrink Archimedes' Screw, Boost Conductivity 100-Fold</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#molecular motor</span> <span>#Archimedes screw</span> <span>#electrical conductivity</span> <span>#molecular electronics</span></div>
       <p>Scientists have engineered a molecular motor, inspired by the ancient Archimedes' screw, that operates using electricity and can spin in either direction. This groundbreaking device, drawing inspiration from a machine invented over 2,000 years ago, also remarkably boosts electrical conductivity by up to 100 times, signaling a significant advance for molecular electronics.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-01/scientists-shrink-archimedes-screw-boost-conductivity-100-fo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Prof. Michael Pittelkow's group at the University of Copenhagen developed the central component: a spiral molecule measuring only a few millionths of a millimeter, designed to attach to gold surfaces via sulfur atoms. At the Weizmann Institute of Science in Israel, Prof. Oren Tal and Dr. Anil Kumar Singh positioned individual molecules between atomically sharp gold tips. Their experiments demonstrated that applying an electrical voltage caused the molecule to rotate, and reversing the voltage's polarity changed its direction. This marks the first demonstration of an electrical current-driven molecular motor, firmly connected to two metal electrodes, with controllable directional rotation.</p><p>The rotation is driven by electrons transferring angular momentum as they travel through the spiral molecule. An unexpected finding was that the rotating molecule conducted electricity far more efficiently than when stationary. Prof. Dr. Fabian Pauly, Léon Martin, and Yuta Ito at the University of Augsburg, using quantum mechanical simulations on the LiCCA supercomputer, discovered that conductivity increases by up to two orders of magnitude because rotation makes highly conductive molecular positions accessible, which the molecule naturally avoids when at rest. Léon Martin explained, &quot;The surprising thing is that it is precisely the positions that the molecule avoids when at rest that conduct electricity best. It is only the rotation that makes these positions accessible.&quot;</p><p>This international collaboration, including theoretical physicists Per Hedegård of the Niels Bohr Institute and Jing-Tao Lü of Huazhong University of Science and Technology, developed a theoretical model that replicated both the observed rotation and current increase. This combined experimental and theoretical approach was crucial for understanding the nanoscale mechanisms, as Yuta Ito stated. The achievement could advance molecular electronic components, spintronics, and microfluidic systems.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is comprehensive, accurately attributing all findings and technical details to the correct individuals and institutions.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
