     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-11-01 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Shops looted as cost of living protests turn violent across German cities</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Germany</span> <span>#Protests</span> <span>#Europe</span></div>
       <p>Protests against the cost of living and other government policies turned violent in several German cities on Saturday, including Frankfurt, Berlin, Cologne, and Hamburg. Police reported that demonstrators targeted emergency services with bottles, stones, and fireworks, leading authorities to deploy water cannons to disperse crowds.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-01/shops-looted-as-cost-of-living-protests-turn-violent-across-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The demonstrations were organized by the campaign group Gen Z regelt. According to spokesperson Lina Friedrich, the protests aimed to demand better support for health and education systems, while opposing compulsory military service and calling for a fairer distribution of economic burdens. While organizers had estimated higher turnouts, authorities in Frankfurt, Munich, and Berlin reported a combined total of approximately 18,000 protesters.</p><p>As night fell, the protests in Frankfurt devolved into riots. Police stated that windows of several shops were smashed, and Molotov cocktails were thrown at emergency personnel. Interior minister of Hesse state, Roman Poseck, stated that approximately 100 left-wing extremists had infiltrated the protest. Officials reported that windows of a gun shop were shattered and two weapons were seized during the unrest, resulting in multiple arrests.</p><p>The German unrest coincided with large-scale demonstrations in France, where more than 320,000 people protested on Saturday. The French interior ministry reported over 500 arrests across cities including Toulouse, Lyon, and Marseille, following clashes where police fired tear gas at demonstrators. Similar protests have recently occurred across a wide range of European nations, including Italy, Portugal, and Belgium, with participants focusing on housing crises, inflation, and public service funding.</p><p>What to watch: Potential government response to the infiltration of protests by extremist groups.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reports on the German protests and their connection to broader European unrest, though it attributes the 100 extremists to the Frankfurt protest specifically, which aligns with the text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqj34gnd3n30o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
