     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-11-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Understanding Fitbit Heart Rate Variability Data</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Fitbit's heart rate variability (HRV) reading, measured in milliseconds, tracks the time difference between heartbeats to provide insights into stress and recovery. Because HRV is highly individual, users should monitor their own trends over time rather than comparing scores against others. Readings are calculated using sleep data from the previous 24 hours via the RMSSD method.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-01/understanding-fitbit-heart-rate-variability-data.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Factors like sleep quality, exercise, and stress influence HRV, and a persistent decline may warrant attention. While Google's Readiness score incorporates HRV to estimate recovery, there is no universal &quot;good&quot; score. Users can track these metrics through the Google Health app, which provides personal ranges based on up to 30 days of historical data.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate summary of the Fitbit HRV measurement process and the importance of individual trend monitoring.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2300292/what-fitbit-heart-rate-variability-tells-you/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
